3.4 Prompts for each stage
These prompts are reference text. Do not run any prompt until its stage is approved. Each prompt assumes that the blueprint and the decisions file are pasted into the session first. Replace the bracketed placeholders with your own values. Never type a secret.
Prompt 0, repository skeleton
You are drafting files for a private project named AEGIS XAU. Read the attached docs. Create README.md, .gitignore for a Node.js and MetaTrader project that excludes .env files, keys, and terminal data, and .env.example containing variable names only, with empty values and a comment for each. Do not include any secret, URL with credentials, or sample key. Mark any unknown value as TODO. Output each file separately with its path.
Prompt 1, Firebase rules
Draft firestore.rules for the AEGIS XAU project. Owner-only access, identified by the Firebase Authentication UID stored in an approved configuration value, not hard-coded. Deny by default. Browser clients may read status collections only. Browser clients may not write any collection in this phase. Trading collections are under modes/demo and modes/live; deny any other mode value. No collection group queries. Include comments explaining each rule. Then list every rule test case a developer should write for the Firebase emulator: allowed owner read, denied non-owner read, denied any write, denied unknown mode. Do not invent a UID.
Prompt 2, backend skeleton
Draft a TypeScript backend skeleton with these modules only: config/env.ts that reads and validates required variables and fails at startup if any is missing; auth/verifyOwner.ts that verifies a Firebase ID token and checks the owner identifier from configuration; api/status.ts, read-only; api/disabled.ts that returns HTTP 403 with a JSON reason for every command and settings endpoint. Do not implement any trading function. Do not write any Firestore write path in this stage. Include unit tests for missing config, invalid token, non-owner token, and disabled endpoints. State which tests you expect to pass and which depend on the emulator.
Prompt 3, telemetry receiver
Draft telemetry/receive.ts, telemetry/validate.ts, crypto/verify.ts, and crypto/replay.ts. Requirements: reject bad signatures, unknown key IDs, mismatched instance or mode, expired messages, timestamps outside the configured tolerance, repeated message IDs, and non-increasing sequence numbers. Store replay state in Firestore through repo.ts only. Do not choose a signature algorithm: read it from configuration and leave a clearly marked TODO referencing decision OS-11. Write one unit test per rejection type.
Prompt 4, dashboard
Draft a read-only React dashboard with the views listed in the file structure. Every trading value must display its mode label and its freshness. Brain views must show NOT IMPLEMENTED until a status field says otherwise. Do not include any control that writes data, except alert acknowledgement if the decision DB-2 is approved. Provide screenshot-friendly layouts for phone and desktop widths. Do not hard-code any sample value that looks like live data; use an empty state.
Prompt 5, EA read-only
Draft an MQL5 Expert Advisor with the include files in the file structure. Phase 1 scope: read symbol specifications, account trade mode, and closed bars on M15, M5, and M1; send signed telemetry; write local logs. The EA must not call OrderSend, OrderSendAsync, PositionModify, PositionClose, or any trade function. Add a compile-time guard that fails if trade functions are referenced. Use only broker-documented properties. Mark every property you are not certain exists as TODO for verification in MetaEditor. Do not invent a minimum stop distance. Provide the compile instructions for MetaEditor.
3.5 How to inspect generated code
1.  Read each file in full. Do not accept a file you have not read.
2.  Search for trading functions in the EA: OrderSend, OrderSendAsync, PositionModify, PositionClose, CTrade. In Phase 1 there must be none.
3.  Search for literal values: keys, tokens, passwords, account numbers, project IDs with credentials, and URLs containing credentials.
4.  Check every TODO against section 12, and every UNRESOLVED decision against DECISIONS.md.
5.  Check that no default is presented as an approved value.
6.  Check imports and paths against the file structure in section 2.
3.6 How to detect missing files
Compare the repository tree against section 2. For each missing file, record in docs/AUDIT_LOG.md whether it is intentionally deferred or a defect. Run the build and the compile. Any error that names a missing file is a defect, not a reason to create a placeholder that hides it.
3.7 How to correct errors
• Paste the exact error text, the file path, and the full file into the session. Do not paraphrase the error.
• Ask for the smallest change that fixes it, and forbid changes outside the named file.
• Reject any fix that removes a security check, a test, or a rejection rule to make a test pass.
• Record each correction and its reason in the audit log.
3.8 How to verify each stage
Each stage has three checks: the automated tests in its section 9 list pass with recorded output; a manual review against the acceptance criteria in section 10; and a check that nothing outside the stage's scope was changed. A stage passes only when all three are recorded.
