1.  Sign in to the Firebase console with the Founder's Google account. Use an account with two-factor authentication enabled.
2.  Create one project. Name it with a neutral identifier. Do not include the word production, broker, or any account number.
3.  Choose the default resource location at creation. Firestore location cannot be changed later (NOT VERIFIED). Recommended: the region closest to the backend host (OS-2).
4.  Enable Google Analytics only if you want it. It is not required.
5.  Record the project ID in docs/DECISIONS.md. Do not record any key or credential.
Billing: some services used by a backend, including Cloud Functions and Cloud Run, require the Blaze pay-as-you-go plan (NOT VERIFIED). Upgrading is a manual financial decision and needs a budget alert set in Google Cloud Billing before deployment.
4.2 Authentication (MANUAL configuration, generated rules)
1.  In Authentication, enable one sign-in method only. Recommended: Google sign-in, or email and password with multi-factor enabled (DB-6 decides the method).
2.  Create the owner account manually. Record its UID in backend configuration as OWNER_UID. The UID is an identifier, not a secret, but keep it out of the public repository in the first stage.
3.  Prevent new account creation. Method depends on the product tier: Firebase Authentication may offer a setting to disable signups, and some controls require Identity Platform (NOT VERIFIED). If not available, rely on server-side owner checks and the rule that only OWNER_UID is accepted.
4.  Enable multi-factor authentication for the owner account if the chosen method supports it (NOT VERIFIED for each method).
5.  Set authorised domains to the dashboard domain only.
4.3 Cloud Firestore (MANUAL creation, generated schema)
1.  Create the database in production mode, so that default-open rules are never active. Do not use test mode.
2.  Collections and document shapes are proposed in section 4.3 of the schema below. Create no collections by hand; the backend creates them.
3.  Enable point-in-time recovery only after checking availability for the chosen edition (NOT VERIFIED).
Schema (PROPOSED, from V4 section 10):
• status/current: single document with component status, each field with timestamp and freshness.
• modes/demo/... and modes/live/...: decisions, brain_outputs, risk_events, commands, order_requests, executions, trades, analytics, telemetry_status, strategy_runs. Each document has a mode field and a source field.
• config/global: non-trading preferences.
• config/modes/demo and config/modes/live: mode-specific settings, versioned.
• admin_audit: owner actions, no trading figures.
• system: component health.
• replay: message IDs and sequence state for the telemetry channel. Backend-only.
4.4 Storage (OPTIONAL, default not used)
Use Cloud Storage only if reports or exported files must be stored. Default: not enabled. If enabled, rules deny all access except backend service identity. Storage must not hold trading records that are also in Firestore.
4.5 Security rules (GENERATED, MANUALLY REVIEWED, DEPLOYED BY CLI)
• Drafted in Stage 1 (section 3.4, prompt 1).
• Principles: deny by default; owner-only reads by OWNER_UID; no browser writes in Phase 1; mode segment restricted to demo and live; no collection group queries.
• Tested in the Firebase Emulator Suite before any deployment. Test output recorded in docs/AUDIT_LOG.md.
• Deployment: firebase deploy --only firestore:rules, run by the Founder after Phase 1 approval.
4.6 Cloud Functions or backend runtime (DECISION OS-2)
The backend may run as Cloud Functions, Cloud Run, or a separate managed server. This is UNRESOLVED (OS-2). Each option needs its own deployment and billing check. Recommended for review: a single containerised service, because a long-running poller is simpler to reason about than many functions. NOT VERIFIED for cost and cold-start behaviour.
4.7 App Check (RECOMMENDED, configure after dashboard exists)
App Check helps block requests that do not come from your app. It does not replace owner authentication and must never be treated as an authorisation control. Configure for the dashboard web app and for Firestore once the dashboard runs. NOT VERIFIED for current provider setup.
4.8 Indexes
Generated by the CLI when a query requires a composite index. Keep firestore.indexes.json in the repository. Do not add indexes speculatively.
4.9 Environment variables and secrets
• Public values that may appear in dashboard code: Firebase web configuration (API key, project ID, app ID). These identify the project. They are not secrets and do not grant access. Security is enforced by rules and owner checks.
• Private values that must never be in the repository: service-account credentials, backend signing private key, telemetry keys, any token.
• Storage: backend secrets in the cloud secret manager, read at runtime by the service identity. The EA's keys stay on the trading host in restricted files (V4 section 9).
• .env.example lists names only. A developer copies it to .env on their own machine. The .env file is ignored by git.
4.10 Backups
• Firestore managed export, scheduled, to a storage location with restricted access (NOT VERIFIED for setup steps and cost).
• The trading host keeps its own copies of EA state files, and the owner copies them off the host on a schedule (OS-9).
• Restore procedure is a required artifact before DEMO. It is not yet written (OS-12).
4.11 Monitoring
• Cloud Logging for backend and function logs. Retention to be set (OS-5).
• Alert policies for: missing telemetry beyond the heartbeat limit (EA-5); rejected signatures above a threshold; authentication failures above a threshold; backend error rate. Thresholds are UNRESOLVED until values are set.
• Alert channel: email to the owner at minimum. Other channels are optional.
4.12 Deployment (manual approval gates)
1.  Emulator tests pass.
2.  Founder approves the stage.
3.  Founder runs the deploy command for rules, indexes, and backend, one at a time, reviewing the output of each.
4.  Post-deploy check: unauthenticated and non-owner requests are refused. Recorded in the audit log.
