5. Complete twelve-Brain implementation
Labels: identities are APPROVED. Each Brain's specification, class, inputs, and tests are PROPOSED. No Brain is implemented or tested. Every Brain has the same common rules, stated once here and applied to each.
5.0 Common rules for all twelve
• Output record: each Brain writes one output per evaluation with brain ID, spec version, status (OK, STALE, DATA INSUFFICIENT, ERROR, NOT IMPLEMENTED), timestamps of every input used, a hash of the inputs, the result, and a reason string.
• Failure behaviour: missing, stale, or invalid input gives DATA INSUFFICIENT or STALE. A Brain never guesses to fill a gap.
• Decision authority: no Brain sends orders, changes stops, or changes settings. Only the Decision Engine combines outputs, and only the EA acts.
• Communication: in-process call inside the backend. The output is written to modes/{mode}/brain_outputs through repo.ts. Brain 12 placement is UNRESOLVED (BA-1).
• Versioning: every change to a specification creates a new version. Old outputs keep their version.
• Testing before status IMPLEMENTED: unit tests on fixed inputs, golden-output tests, failure-state tests, and a repaint test for any rule using closed bars. Out-of-sample evidence is required for any output that affects a decision.
• Confidence: no probability is displayed until calibrated on out-of-sample data.
Brain 01: Macro and Fundamental Intelligence
• Purpose: give event and rate context for Gold.
• Responsibilities: classify verified scheduled events by impact; mark event windows; flag unknown windows.
• Inputs: timestamped economic calendar from a verified source (source UNRESOLVED, BA-5 and licence review).
• Outputs: event list with impact class, window start and end, source, and status.
• Class: deterministic classification; statistical study of event windows, later.
• Dependencies: news source, BA-5.
• Failure behaviour: source unavailable gives STALE, and the Engine treats the window as unknown (BA-5).
• Testing: parsing tests on sample calendar files, timezone tests, missing-source tests.
Brain 02: Market Regime Intelligence
• Purpose: classify the current market as trend, range, high or low volatility, or transition.
• Responsibilities: compute regime from closed bars using defined thresholds.
• Inputs: closed M15 bars; ADX and EMA computed from them.
• Outputs: regime label, indicator values, bar timestamps.
• Class: deterministic thresholds, with statistical calibration on history.
• Dependencies: Market data from EA (section 6).
• Failure behaviour: fewer bars than the indicator needs gives DATA INSUFFICIENT.
• Testing: golden tests on fixed bar sets; threshold sensitivity tests; repaint test.
Brain 03: Cross-Market Correlation Intelligence
• Purpose: report the relationship between XAUUSD and DXY, yields, and correlated instruments.
• Responsibilities: align series by timestamp; compute rolling correlation with a stability check; flag breakdowns.
• Inputs: DXY, bond yields, and other series. Sources UNRESOLVED (BA-6 and licence review).
• Outputs: correlation values, window length, stability flag, breakdown flag.
• Class: statistical.
• Dependencies: BA-6; alignment with EA bar times.
• Failure behaviour: missing series gives DATA INSUFFICIENT for the affected pair only.
• Testing: known-series tests, alignment tests across time zones, breakdown-detection tests.
Brain 04: Liquidity Mapping Intelligence
• Purpose: map liquidity levels and detect sweeps.
• Responsibilities: equal highs and lows, session and day and week highs and lows, swing points; sweep detection with wick, close, and reclaim rules (supporting concept held here).
• Inputs: closed bars on M15 and M5; session windows from Brain 10.
• Outputs: level list with type and source timestamp; sweep events with the rule that matched.
• Class: deterministic.
• Dependencies: Brain 10 for session boundaries; Brain 06 for swing definitions if shared.
• Failure behaviour: no session data gives DATA INSUFFICIENT for session-based levels only.
• Testing: fixed-chart level tests, sweep rule tests with positive and negative examples, repaint test.
Brain 05: Order Flow Intelligence
• Purpose: report pressure and imbalance evidence from available volume.
• Responsibilities: compute bar-based pressure measures from broker tick volume.
• Inputs: closed bars with tick volume from the broker.
• Outputs: pressure values, imbalance flags, data-source label stating tick volume.
• Class: deterministic.
• Dependencies: EA bar reading.
• Failure behaviour: zero or missing volume gives DATA INSUFFICIENT.
• Limitation: tick volume is not global institutional volume. Every output must carry that label.
• Testing: fixed-bar tests; label-presence tests.
Brain 06: Market Structure Intelligence
• Purpose: report swings and structure state.
• Responsibilities: swing detection by fixed rule; BOS, CHOCH, and market structure shift (supporting concepts held here).
• Inputs: closed bars on M15 and M5.
• Outputs: swing list, structure state, event list with rule IDs.
• Class: deterministic.
• Dependencies: none beyond bars.
• Failure behaviour: insufficient swings give DATA INSUFFICIENT.
• Testing: fixed-chart structure tests; repaint test; definition-version test.
Brain 07: Volatility Intelligence
• Purpose: report volatility level and changes.
• Responsibilities: ATR, bar range, expansion and contraction flags, unusual movement flag.
• Inputs: closed bars on M1, M5, M15.
• Outputs: ATR values, range ratios, flags.
• Class: deterministic and statistical.
• Dependencies: bars.
• Failure behaviour: missing bars give DATA INSUFFICIENT.
• Testing: known-value tests, threshold tests.
Brain 08: Momentum and Displacement Intelligence
• Purpose: detect strong directional movement and momentum.
• Responsibilities: body-to-range ratio, body versus ATR, displacement flag (supporting concept held here).
• Inputs: closed bars; ATR from Brain 07.
• Outputs: displacement events, momentum values, rule IDs.
• Class: deterministic.
• Dependencies: Brain 07.
• Failure behaviour: missing ATR gives DATA INSUFFICIENT.
• Testing: fixed-bar tests with positive and negative cases; repaint test.
Brain 09: SMC Setup Intelligence
• Purpose: combine liquidity, structure, and displacement into setup candidates.
• Responsibilities: order blocks, fair value gaps, premium and discount from a defined dealing range (supporting concepts held here); setup candidate with its rule trail.
• Inputs: outputs of Brains 04, 06, 08; closed bars.
• Outputs: setup candidates with reasons and rule IDs; zone list with status (candidate, mitigated, invalid).
• Class: deterministic rules. Setup quality is a hypothesis until tested.
• Dependencies: Brains 04, 06, 08.
• Failure behaviour: any missing upstream output gives DATA INSUFFICIENT for the setup.
• Testing: rule tests per concept; combination tests; out-of-sample evidence before any setup affects a decision.
Brain 10: Session Intelligence
• Purpose: report the trading session and its boundaries.
• Responsibilities: Asian, London, New York windows; overlaps; broker time mapping; daylight-saving handling.
• Inputs: broker server time; session definitions (to be fixed in the specification).
• Outputs: current session, time to next boundary, overlap flag.
• Class: deterministic.
• Dependencies: EA time source; OS-1 for host clock.
• Failure behaviour: unknown broker offset gives DATA INSUFFICIENT and blocks session-dependent entries.
• Testing: boundary tests across daylight-saving changes; offset-change tests.
Brain 11: Execution and Trade Quality Intelligence
• Purpose: report execution conditions and a quality measure.
• Responsibilities: spread, slippage, rejection, and latency statistics; a quality score marked advisory.
• Inputs: broker quotes from the EA; execution records.
• Outputs: spread values, slippage statistics, rejection counts, quality score with calibration status.
• Class: statistical; advisory. The hard gate is EA-local only (BA-3).
• Dependencies: EA execution records (Phase 3 for execution data).
• Failure behaviour: insufficient execution data gives DATA INSUFFICIENT for the score; the EA-local spread and stop checks still apply.
• Testing: statistic tests on fixed records; calibration tests before any use.
Brain 12: Trade Management Intelligence
• Purpose: propose stop management and exit actions.
• Responsibilities: propose break-even, trailing stop, partial close, and management exits under approved parameters.
• Inputs: position state from reconciliation; broker stop levels; Brain outputs; approved management parameters.
• Outputs: proposals only, each as a class R action with a reason. Never an order.
• Class: deterministic.
• Decision authority: none. Proposals are executed only by the EA, within the class R rules in V4 section 6.
• Dependencies: BA-1 (location), BA-2 (fallback), Phase 3 only.
• Failure behaviour: Brain 12 unavailable means no management changes. Existing broker stops remain. This is the V4.1 rule. Never imply management is still working when it is not.
• Communication: if the EA runs it (recommended, BA-1), management is local, and the backend only receives its state. If the backend runs it, proposals use the authenticated command channel.
• Testing: rule tests on fixed position states; stop-direction tests (a stop must never widen risk); broker stop-distance tests once B4 is verified; failure-state tests.
Decision Engine (supporting, not a Brain)
Combines outputs into BUY candidate, SELL candidate, WAIT, or REJECT, with reasons. Depends on all twelve. Tested for every output state and for conflicting Brain outputs.
6. MT5 Expert Advisor architecture
Labels: this architecture is PROPOSED. Function names and MT5 properties are drawn from MQL5 documentation and must be verified in MetaEditor before reliance (NOT VERIFIED for every name listed). The EA is the core product and the only order sender.
Phase boundary: Phase 1 contains reading, telemetry, and logging only. No trade function is present in Phase 1 code (section 3.4, prompt 5).
6.1 Initialisation (OnInit)
1.  Read input parameters from Config.mqh. Validate ranges. Refuse to start on invalid input and write the reason to the log.
2.  Read the account trade mode. Record whether the account is netting or hedging (B3, NOT VERIFIED). Refuse to start if the mode is unrecognised.
3.  Read the account mode, DEMO or LIVE, from the account properties. Refuse to start if the mode does not match the configured mode. LIVE is refused in every phase covered here.
4.  Match the configured symbol to the broker symbol, including any suffix. Refuse if no exact match is found.
5.  Read symbol specifications: digits, point, tick size, tick value, contract size, volume min, max, and step, stop level and freeze level (B4, NOT VERIFIED).
6.  Load persisted state from the EA state file: risk totals, streak, pause expiry, last sequence, command IDs seen, request references.
7.  Check the system clock against the time source. Mark the clock status.
8.  Run startup reconciliation (6.8) before enabling anything.
9.  Start the timer used for telemetry and command polling.
6.2 Broker connection
• The EA reads connection state each tick and on a timer.
• Disconnect: the EA sets DISCONNECTED, sends nothing, and keeps existing broker stops in place.
• Reconnect: the EA reconciles before any class N action is allowed.
• Allowed external URLs for telemetry and command polling must be listed in the terminal's options. The list is documented in setup (B5, NOT VERIFIED).
6.3 Market data
• The EA reads closed bars only on M15, M5, and M1. The current forming bar is never used for a decision.
• Each bar is stored with its open time, and the broker time offset is recorded.
• Ticks are read only for spread and quote checks, not for signals.
• Data freshness: if the latest closed bar is older than the timeframe allows, the EA marks the timeframe STALE.
6.4 Strategy execution
• Phase 1: none.
• Phase 2: strategies run in the Strategy Tester and the research harness only. No broker order.
• Phase 3: an approved strategy produces a candidate, which arrives as a class N command. The EA does not generate its own entries unless the Founder approves that design. This is a point to confirm (not yet decided).
6.5 Risk and Safety Supervisor
The Supervisor is the only gate for class N actions. It is described in V4 section 7 and the Founder Decisions document. Its checks, in order, are: state is RECONCILED; no pause, breach, suspension, or BACKEND LOST state; mode is DEMO and matches the account; single-position rule passes; per-trade risk, daily and weekly loss, and consecutive losses pass; session, volatility, news, and execution-quality gates pass; stop-loss is valid. Any failure refuses the order and logs the reason.
Blocked until decided: the accounting base (CS-1 to CS-6, AR-1 to AR-6). Until then the Supervisor refuses all class N actions. This is a designed fail-closed state, not a trading strategy.
6.6 Order execution
• Each order request carries a request reference stored in the EA state file before the request is sent.
• The EA sets a stop-loss with every order. An order with no valid stop is refused before sending.
• The EA uses the terminal's standard trade functions. Results are read from the returned status and then confirmed from history, not accepted from the send result alone.
• The EA sets a magic number on every request so that its positions can be identified.
• Comment fields have length limits (NOT VERIFIED for the current build). Keep the request reference in the state file, and keep the comment short.
• Timed-out or unclear results: the EA searches open orders, positions, and recent deals for the reference. It does not resend blindly.
6.7 Stop-loss enforcement and position management
• Enforcement: a position without a valid stop, opened by the EA, triggers the EC-3 check. Correction is attempted first, under the stop rules in V4 section 6 (a stop may only move in the protective direction, and must respect the broker minimum distance).
• Mandatory local protections (always on): stop on every order; broker-held stops; local risk state; reconciliation; fail-closed on unknown state; clock-status refusal of class N.
• Optional Brain 12 management (Phase 3 only, BA-1): break-even, trailing stop, partial close. If Brain 12 is unavailable or disabled, none of these runs. Existing stops stay as set.
• Position management never acts on manual positions (EC-6).
6.8 Broker history reconciliation
• At startup and after any disconnect, the EA compares its state file with open positions, pending orders, and deal history for its magic number and request references.
• Match: state becomes RECONCILED.
• Any unmatched EA position or order, or any missing record, sets RECONCILE REQUIRED. Class N is blocked. Class R is allowed only for identified positions.
• Manual changes detected in history are recorded as EXTERNAL MANUAL (EA-7, V4.1 section 2).
• Reconciliation uses broker history only. The backend copy is not authoritative.
6.9 Duplicate-order prevention
• Request reference, stored before sending.
• Command IDs seen are stored and checked; a repeated command returns its stored result.
• Single-position rule checked from live terminal state immediately before sending (CS-7, CS-8, CS-9).
6.10 Emergency closure
Two paths, kept separate (Founder Decisions, EC section):
• Owner-requested: a signed class R command from the backend, after owner authentication, recent re-authentication, and two-step confirmation. Available from Phase 3 only (EC-1, EC-2).
• EA-detected critical fault: the EA closes its own identified position only when a fault on the approved trigger list persists for the approved check count (EC-3, EC-4). No backend decision is needed.
• Both paths: close only identified EA positions; use broker-confirmed results; retry up to the approved count (EC-5); escalate to a critical alert and a class N block when the close is not confirmed.
• Both paths: no opposite opening trade to offset; on netting accounts, a reducing order of the exact volume is used under EA-1.
• Manual human closure in the terminal or mobile application is separate. It is always permitted, recorded as external, and reconciled.
6.11 Telemetry
• Message contents: instance ID, mode, sequence, sent and expiry times, field-level timestamps, heartbeat, account mode, symbol status, bar status, clock status, EA state, pause and risk totals (read-only copies).
• Signed per section 9 of V4 (signing scheme OS-11, NOT VERIFIED).
• Telemetry sends are retried with the same message ID. The backend accepts a repeat without a second write.
• In Phase 1, telemetry is the only outbound channel.
6.12 Error handling
• Each error has a code, a severity, and an action.
• Critical: state corruption, identity mismatch, clock failure with a position open, failed close. Action: block class N; raise alert; for EC-3 faults, follow the emergency path.
• Warning: a single failed send, a stale bar, a telemetry failure. Action: log; retry within limits.
• Logging: local log files with rotation (retention OS-5). Log entries are not secrets and never contain keys.
6.13 Recovery after restart
1.  Load state file. If unreadable or missing: fail closed, set RECONCILE REQUIRED, and do not infer state from memory.
2.  Reconcile against broker history (6.8).
3.  Re-check pause expiry against broker time.
4.  Resume telemetry.
5.  Resume class N only when all Supervisor checks pass.
