• Service identities: backend service identity for Firestore writes; no other principal has write access to trading paths.
• EA identity: instance ID plus key ID, checked against the registered instance list.
• Phase 1 authorisation: read-only. All command and settings endpoints refuse with a stated reason.
8.4 API security
• HTTPS only.
• Input validation on every endpoint: schema, length, type, allowed values.
• Rate limiting per identity and per endpoint (mechanism to be chosen; NOT VERIFIED for the chosen host).
• Error responses do not reveal internal state, keys, or stack traces.
• CORS limited to the dashboard origin.
• Dependency scanning in CI for known vulnerabilities.
8.5 Command signing and telemetry signing
• Preferred: asymmetric signatures. The backend signs commands with a private key held in the secret manager. The EA holds only verification keys (OS-11, subject to B1, NOT VERIFIED).
• Fallback: HMAC-SHA256 with a per-instance key. Limitation: a key present on the host can forge messages for that instance. Use only with a recorded risk acceptance.
• Telemetry: EA-side signing key is per instance, stored in a restricted file on the host. Its compromise lets an attacker feed false telemetry. The EA's own checks and the Supervisor still limit damage.
• Each message includes: key ID, instance ID, mode, sequence, issued time, expiry, nonce, payload hash, signature.
• Verification order: key ID known and not revoked; signature valid; instance and mode match; not expired; time within tolerance; sequence greater than last applied; ID not seen before. Any failure is logged with the reason.
8.6 Replay prevention
• Backend: message IDs stored for at least the expiry window plus margin; repeats rejected.
• EA: last sequence and command IDs stored in the state file; repeats return the stored result without re-execution.
• Sequence reset is an owner procedure on the host only, logged (OS-9).
8.7 Key management
• Backend signing key: generated in the secret manager or a managed key service, never exported to the repository or to a developer machine where avoidable. Access limited to the backend service identity.
• Rotation: new key ID added to the EA's accepted set on the host, then backend switches, then old key removed after an overlap window (OS-8, values UNRESOLVED).
• Revocation: backend stops signing with the key; EA set updated by the owner on the host; backend suspension is immediate.
• Telemetry keys: generated by the owner, stored on the host, registered at the backend. Revoked on compromise.
• Key generation, storage, and rotation events are recorded in admin_audit without the key value.
8.8 Secrets management
• Secret manager: backend secrets, read at runtime.
• Repository: no secrets, ever. .gitignore and a pre-commit scan are required. Pre-commit scanning tool to be chosen (NOT VERIFIED).
• Developer machine: secrets kept in the operating system credential store or a password manager. Never in prompts, chat, or documents.
• Trading host: EA keys in restricted-permission files. The account password is entered in the terminal by the owner and is not stored in any project file.
• Rotation schedule: UNRESOLVED (OS-8).
8.9 Audit logs
• admin_audit: owner actions, authentication events, setting proposals, key events, suspension and emergency requests. Append-only by rule. Readable by owner only. No trading figures.
• Trading records: decisions, order requests, executions, trades, risk events. Written only by the backend service identity. Mode-scoped.
• EA local logs: rotated files, retention OS-5. Reconciled against broker history at review.
• Log integrity: periodic export of logs to a separate restricted location (OS-5, OS-12).
8.10 Firebase rules
• Deny by default.
• Owner read only on status and mode-scoped paths, by UID.
• No browser writes to any trading path in Phase 1.
• Mode segment must be demo or live.
• No collection group queries.
• Rule tests in the emulator: owner read allowed; non-owner read denied; any write denied; unknown mode denied; no unauthenticated access.
